Shift Fitting under Heteroskedasticity
====================================

Open index.html directly, or serve this repository as static files. Keep
../TeachingTools/statistics.js, browser.js and browser.css at their relative
paths. No build, packages, remote assets, Shiny server or network is required.
app.R is unchanged and retained as the historical reference.

Lecture:
https://anguswright.github.io/LectureNotes/IntroductionToStatistics_Section4b_html.html
Gallery: ../

The experiment
--------------
Truth is y=x or the unit-normal density. Each observation has independent
additive Gaussian noise with known SD: constant (arithmetic mean of the two SD
endpoints), linear in SD, or log-linear in SD across an evenly spaced x grid.
Logarithmic sample/repetition controls are rounded to the nearest integer.
The same local seed reproduces the same observations. Data controls are staged:
click Generate data to apply them. Every fixed model uses the same realisations;
fit and display controls never resimulate data.

Fit y=m*x+b with fixed slope m, or A*N(x;mu,s) with fixed width s.
Report shifts b or mu, respectively (both true zero), and Gaussian amplitudes.
Every realisation is fitted. The data panel shows the selected realisation and
its SD bars, fitted curves and up to 100 deterministic ensemble curves per model.
The other panels show normalised shift histograms and shift versus width/slope.

Translation decisions and mathematical repairs
----------------------------------------------
* The R objective divides squared residuals by SD, not variance. This legacy
  weighting remains the default and is labelled. Equal weights and the correct
  known-noise Gaussian likelihood weights 1/SD^2 are explicit alternatives.
* The original "correction" is preserved as an optional two-pass heuristic:
  regress absolute residuals on fitted response, multiply base weights by
  inverse squared predicted residual size, and refit. It does NOT estimate the
  known noise model reliably, particularly for a symmetric Gaussian response.
  Nonpositive predicted scales are rejected, never clipped. Its R linear call
  used a nonexistent width argument; the translation correctly holds slope fixed.
* Gaussian amplitude is profiled analytically (unrestricted, as in R). A grid
  resolves centroid peaks and local golden-section searches refine them. The
  centroid is explicitly restricted to the sampled x interval, rather than one
  unconstrained local optim() start. Boundary and singular uncertainties are
  unavailable. This is a finite numerical search, not a proof of global optimality.
* Data are paired across models, unlike the independent draws in each R loop.
  This supports meaningful fixed-data comparisons and avoids resimulation on
  figure/annotation changes. Up to 100 curves are drawn without R's failure when
  sampling 100 curves from fewer than 100 realisations. All simulation results
  enter summaries and plots except the explicitly labelled ensemble-curve cap.
* Linear intercept propagation is exact for fixed weights:
      Var(b) = sum(w_i^2 * sigma_i^2) / sum(w_i)^2.
  Nonlinear propagation uses H^-1 B H^-1 with B=J'W diag(sigma^2) WJ and the
  full objective Hessian H=J'WJ-sum(w_i*r_i*second_derivative(f_i)).
  Dropping the residual-curvature term would be wrong under misspecification.
  These Gaussian intervals are local approximations, not guaranteed 95% coverage.
  Width/slope uncertainty is never included: these are controlled fixed values.
* Conditional SD uses the generating noise, even under legacy/equal fit weights.
  It does not pretend those weights describe the true likelihood. No analytic
  SD/coverage is claimed for the data-dependent residual heuristic. Its reference
  remains the noiseless BASE-weight optimum (noiseless residuals are not a noise
  sample). The known truth has no estimation uncertainty.
* Mean shift, Monte Carlo SE of that mean, empirical shift SD, noiseless shift
  and hypothetical noise propagation around the noiseless reference are separate.
  Coverage reports both true zero and the noiseless reference, with the actual
  interval denominator; unavailable/boundary intervals are excluded and counted.
  There is no empirical SD or Monte Carlo SE from only one realisation.
* Bias–Width bands use paired realisation-specific regression trends, not
  ordinary regression treating model cells as independent. Inner bands are
  +/-1.96 MC SE of the mean trend, outer bands +/-1.96 SD of realisation trends.
  These are descriptive normal approximations, not exact confidence/prediction
  intervals. No trend bands for one realisation or identical model parameters.
* R's unimplemented Poissonian/Gamma entries remain explicitly unavailable;
  no new count-response model is silently substituted. There was no animation.
  Axes adapt to finite results rather than clipping observations to R's hard
  y limits. Annotation scaling adjusts plot headings and legends.

Validation
----------
From the repository root, using Node 22 or later:
  node --test ShiftFitting/test_shift.js
  node ShiftFitting/test_browser.js

The browser suite uses ../TeachingTools/browser_test.js and its local Chrome
harness; it adds no npm dependency. It checks all display/noise/weight branches,
linear and Gaussian fits, residual correction, fixed-data comparisons, invalid
inputs, one-realisation behaviour, mobile DPR/layout and direct file opening.
Optional screenshots are controlled by HTML_SCREENSHOT_DIR in that harness.

Explicit finite-work limits prevent accidental browser lockups: at most
1,000,000 stored observations and an estimated 60,000,000 fit work units.
Large combinations fail with a visible message; they never silently downsample.
Gaussian fits need at least three x samples; linear fits need at least two.
